/**

@page benchmark_multigrid_petsc_hdg Mixed Possion equation with PETSc

Here we discuss the steps involved in running the file benchmarks/multigrid/petsc/hdg.py.
We will try and discuss some of the mathematics behind the solver, but we mainly focus on
how to build and run it.

For information on how to build Peano with PETSc, see \ref multigrid_home.

## Mixed Poisson / Hybridised Discontinuous Galerkin

At time of writing, the actual focus of this part of the repo has not yet been fixed. We
aim to provide a fairly general solver, which we describe here.

## Cell and Face Degrees of Freedom

Taking some inspiration from DG schemes, we fix the number of degrees of freedom on the 
cells and the faces by the degree of our desired polynomial. In particular, for 
polynomal degree @f$ n @f$ and spatial dimension @f$ d @f$, we set up our equation to 
have:

@f$  (n + 1) ^d  @f$ degrees of freedom on each face (hereafter referred to as faceDofs), and

@f$  ( n + 1 )^ {d-1}  @f$ degrees of freedom on each cell (hereafter referred to as cellDofs).

At time of writing, we fix @f$ n @f$ to be 1, and @f$ d @f$ to be 2.

## What does the code do?

Much of the setup is hidden from the user. It's worth mentioning that Peano traverses across the mesh,
assigining global indices to the cells and faces. Cells take indices from 0 to cellDofs -1,
and Faces take indices from cellDofs to cellDofs + faceDofs - 1. We also initialise a square 
matrix, which has (cellDofs + faceDofs) rows. This will capture our "problem", and we describe
how it is filled below. We also initialise vectors for LHS and RHS, both of which need filling
in by the user.

The main thing that happens during the Assembly routine, is that stencils of certain size are 
inserted into the problem matrix above. Broken down into some steps, we:

\li Traverse through the mesh, stopping in at each cell
\li Inside each cell, we gather all the global indices associated with the @f$ 2d @f$ faces, as well as the
    global indices associated with each of the cells.
\li We use these indices to insert stencils at the appropriate location in the problem matrix

## What do we use for stencils? Is this the correct terminology?

The stencils can be provided by the user. At time of writing, we include some stencils
which pertain to a mixed poisson problem, considering velocities and pressures. We accept stencils for:

\li Interactions between degrees of freedom within a cell
\li Interactions between degrees of freedom on a face
\li Interactions between degrees of freedom on a cell, with those on each of the @f$ 2d @f$ faces within that cell
\li Interactions between degrees of freedom on the @f$ 2d @f$ faces with the cell.

Note that the stencils for the final two mentioned here may indeed be transposes of each other. In general, they
may be different, and so we treat them as such.

We note the dimensions for each of the stencils mentioned above:

\li The stencil should contain cellDofs rows, cellDofs columns.
\li The stencil should contain faceDofs rows, faceDofs columns.
\li The stencil should contain cellDofs rows, faceDofs * 2d columns.
\li The stencil should contain faceDofs * 2d rows, cellDofs columns.

The reason for the dimensions of the last two is that in general, there is a different stencil for interactions between the face and the cell, depending on whether the face is at the left, bottom,
right or top of the cell. So, following the enumeration order outlined in @ref multigrid_enumeration,
the first faceDofs columns are reserved for the stencil that governs the interactions between the
left face and the cell, the next columns for the bottom stencil, etc in the order left, bottom, 
right, top.

## Insert a custom stencil

The user is free to insert a stencil for a problem that suits them in the file benchmarks/multigrid/petsc/hdg.py. There is no guarantee it will do something physical!

As an example, we show how to insert a 3x3 matrix, describing how the face degrees of freedom interact with each other. Note that this is a special case, and the matrix inserted must have dimensions
faceDofs x faceDofs. We write as a list of lists.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
stencilFF=[
  [-0.00987654,  0.00246914, -0.00493827],
  [ 0.00246914, -0.00987654, -0.00493827],
  [-0.00493827, -0.00493827, -0.03950617]
]
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

As a sanity check, len(stencilFF) should be equal to the intended number of rows, and len(stencilFF[0]) 
should be equal to the intended number of columns.


*/